'''Middle Korean''' is the period in the history of the [[Korean language]] succeeding [[Old Korean]] and yielding to [[Early Modern Korean]] in the late 16th century.
The boundary between the Old and Middle periods is traditionally identified with the establishment of [[Goryeo]] in 918 and the associated transition of the prestige dialect from the Southeast to the center of the peninsula, but some scholars have argued for the [[Mongol invasions of Korea]] in the mid-13th century. Middle Korean is divided into Early and Late periods corresponding to Goryeo (until 1392) and [[Joseon]] respectively.

It is difficult to extract linguistic information from texts of the Early period, which are written with [[Chinese characters]] (called [[Hanja]] in Korean). The situation was transformed in 1446 by the introduction of the [[Hangul]] alphabet, so that Late Middle Korean provides the pivotal data for the [[history of Korean]].

== Sources and periodization ==
Middle Korean is traditionally taken to span the period from the establishment of [[Goryeo]] in 918, when the political centre moved from [[Gyeongju]] in the southeast to [[Kaesong]] in the central west, up to the start of the [[Imjin War]] in 1592. The boundary between early and late periods is variously taken as the beginning of the [[Joseon]] period in 1392 or the promulgation of Hangul in 1446.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=77}}{{sfnp|Sohn|2012|p=73}} The Joseon capital was only a short distance away in Hanyang (modern [[Seoul]]), so any language change would have been minimal, but the introduction of Hangul dramatically changed the documentation of the language.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=77, 100}}

Until the late 19th century, most formal writing in Korea, including government documents, scholarship and much literature, was written in [[Classical Chinese]]. Before the 15th century, the little writing in Korean was done using cumbersome systems using Chinese characters, such as {{tlit|ko|[[idu script|idu]]}} and {{tlit|ko|[[hyangchal]]}}. Thus Early Middle Korean, like Old Korean before it, is sparsely documented.{{sfnp|Sohn|2012|p=73}}

Before the 1970s, the key sources for EMK were a few wordlists.
* The ''[[Jilin leishi]]'' (1103–1104) was a Chinese book about Korea. All that survives of the original three volumes is a brief preface and a glossary of over 350 Korean words and phrases.{{sfnp|Yong|Peng|2008|pp=374–375}} The Korean forms were rendered using Chinese characters as phonograms, though sometimes the chosen character had a semantic connection with the Korean term, as is common in Chinese glossing practice. Identification of the Korean pronunciations is complicated by uncertainty about Chinese phonology of the time and the differences between the two languages.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=80–81, 85–86}}
* The {{tlit|zh|Cháoxiǎn guǎn yìyǔ}} ({{lang|zh-Hant|朝鮮館譯語}}, 1408) is another Chinese glossary of Korean, containing 596 Korean words.{{sfnp|Sohn|2015|p=440}}{{sfnp|Ogura|1926|p=2}}
* The {{tlit|ko|Hyangyak kugŭppang}} ({{lang|ko-Hani|朝鮮館譯語鄕藥救急方}}, mid-13th century) is a Korean survey of herbal treatments. The work is written in Chinese, but the Korean names of some 180 ingredients are rendered using Chinese characters according to Korean scribal traditions, using phonograms intended to be read with [[Sino-Korean vocabulary|Sino-Korean]] pronunciations, semantic glosses, and phonograms with native Korean pronunciations.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=81, 86–89}}
* The Japanese text {{tlit|ja|Nichū Reki}} ({{lang|ja-Hani|二中曆}}, believed to be compiled from two works from the early 12th century), contains {{tlit|ja|[[kana]]}} transcriptions of Korean numerals, but is marred by errors.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=81}}

In 1973, a close examination of a [[Buddhist sutra]] from the Goryeo period revealed faint interlinear annotations in simplified Chinese characters indicating how the Chinese text could be read in Korean.
More examples of {{tlit|ko|[[kugyŏl]]}} ('oral embellishment') were discovered, particularly in the 1990s.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=83}}{{sfnp|Nam|2012|pp=46–48}}
Many of the {{tlit|ko|kugyŏl}} characters were abbreviated, and some of them are identical in form and value to symbols in the Japanese [[katakana]] syllabary, though the historical relationship between the two is not yet clear.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=83–84}} An even more subtle method known as {{tlit|ko|kakp'il}} ({{Korean|hangul=각필|hanja=角筆|labels=no|lit='stylus'}}) annotations was discovered in 2000. This consists of dots and lines made with a stylus at various positions around a character, with their interpretation depending on where they are placed.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=84}}{{sfnp|Nam|2012|pp=47–48}} Both forms of annotation contain little phonological information, but are valuable sources on grammatical markers.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=85}}

The {{tlit|ko|kugyŏl}} used after the [[Mongol invasions of Korea]] in the mid-13th century differs from that used before in style and grammar. Nam Pung-hyun suggests that the language changed due to the disruption of the invasions and occupation, and the period before should be considered Late Old Korean rather than part of Middle Korean.{{sfnp|Nam|2012|p=41}}

The introduction of the [[Hangul]] alphabet in 1446 revolutionized the description of the language.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=100}} The {{tlit|ko|[[Hunminjeongeum]]}} ('The Correct/Proper Sounds for the Instruction of the People') and later texts describe the phonology and morphology of the language with great detail and precision.{{sfnp|Sohn|2012|pp=76–77}} Earlier forms of the language must be reconstructed by comparing fragmentary evidence with LMK descriptions.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=100}}
These works are less informative about Korean syntax, as they tend to use a stilted style influenced by Classical Chinese. The best examples of colloquial Korean are the translations in foreign-language textbooks produced by the Joseon [[Bureau of Interpreters]].{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=100}}

== Script and phonology ==
Hangul letters correspond closely to the phonemes of Late Middle Korean. The romanization most commonly used in linguistic writing on the history of Korean is the [[Yale romanization of Korean|Yale romanization]] devised by [[Samuel E. Martin]], which faithfully reflects the Hangul spelling.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=10}}

