The tensed stops ''pp'', ''tt'', ''cc'' and ''kk'' are distinct phonemes in modern Korean, but in LMK they were allophones of consonant clusters.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=128–129}}
The tensed fricative ''hh'' only occurred in a single verb root, {{tlit|okm|hhye-}} 'to pull', and has disappeared in Modern Korean.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=130}}

The voiced fricatives {{IPA|/β/}}, {{IPA|/z/}} and {{IPA|/ɣ/}} occurred only in limited environments, and are believed to have arisen from [[lenition]] of {{IPA|/p/}}, {{IPA|/s/}} and {{IPA|/k/}}, respectively.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=64}}{{sfnp|Whitman|2015|p=431}} They have disappeared in most modern dialects, but some dialects in the southeast and northeast retain {{IPA|/p/}}, {{IPA|/s/}} and {{IPA|/k/}} in these words.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2000|pp=320–321}}

The affricates ''c'', ''ch'' and ''cc'' were apical consonants, as in modern northwestern dialects, rather than palatals as in modern Seoul.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=149–150}}

Late Middle Korean had a limited and skewed set of initial clusters: ''sp-'', ''st-'', ''sk-'', ''pt-'', ''pth-'', ''ps-'', ''pc-'', ''pst-'' and ''psk-''.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=130}}{{sfnp|Cho|Whitman|2019|p=20}} It is believed that they resulted from [[Syncope (phonology)|syncope]] of vowels ''o'' or ''u'' during the Middle Korean period. For example, the {{tlit|lzh|Jilin leishi}} has {{tlit|okm|*posol}} ({{lang|okm-Hant|菩薩}}) 'rice', which became LMK {{tlit|okm|psól}} and modern {{tlit|ko|ssal}}.{{sfnp|Cho|Whitman|2019|pp=19–20}} A similar process is responsible for many aspirated consonants. For example, the {{tlit|lzh|Jilin leishi}} has {{tlit|okm|*huku-}} ({{lang|okm-Hant|黒根}}) 'big', which became LMK and modern {{tlit|ko|khu}}.{{sfnp|Cho|Whitman|2019|p=20}}

