The precise phonetic values of these vowels are controversial.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=156}} Six of them are still distinguished in modern Korean, but only the [[Jeju language]] has a distinct reflex of ''o'', which was written with the now-archaic letter [[araea (letter)|aray a]].{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=156}} In most other varieties it has merged with ''a'' in the first syllable of a word and ''u'' elsewhere.{{sfnp|Sohn|2012|p=81}}
An exception is found in the [[Yukjin Korean]] in the far northeast and in dialects along the south coast, where first-syllable ''o'' has merged with ''wo'' when adjacent to a labial consonant.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2000|pp=319–320}}

