''Yin'' and ''yang'' vowels could not occur in the same word, but could co-occur with the neutral vowel.{{sfnp|Sohn|2012|p=81}}{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=162}}
The phonetic dimension underlying vowel harmony is disputed.
[[Ki-Moon Lee]] suggested that LMK vowel harmony was based on [[vowel height]].{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=162}}
Some recent authors attribute it to [[advanced and retracted tongue root]] states.{{sfnp|Sohn|2015|p=457|loc=n. 4}}

Loans from [[Middle Mongol]] in the 13th century show several puzzling correspondences, in particular between Middle Mongolian ''ü'' and Korean ''u''.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=94}}
Based on these data and transcriptions in the ''Jilin leishi'', Ki-Moon Lee argued for a Korean Vowel Shift between the 13th and 15th centuries, consisting of [[chain shift]]s involving five of these vowels:{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=94–95}}
* {{IPA|y}} > {{IPA|u}} > {{IPA|o}} > {{IPA|ʌ}}
* {{IPA|e}} > {{IPA|ə}} > {{IPA|ɨ}}
[[William Labov]] found that this proposed shift followed different principles to all the other chain shifts he surveyed.{{sfnp|Labov|1994|pp=138–139}}
Lee's interpretation of both the Mongolian and ''Jilin leishi'' materials has also been challenged by several authors.{{sfnp|Whitman|2013|pp=254–255}}{{sfnp|Whitman|2015|p=429}}

LMK also had two [[semivowel|glide]]s, ''y'' {{IPA|[j]}} and ''w'' {{IPA|[w]}}:{{sfnp|Sohn|2012|pp=81–82}}{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=159–161}}
* A ''y'' on-glide could precede four of the vowels, indicated in Hangul with modified letters: ''ya'' {{lang|okm|ㅑ}} {{IPA|[ja]}}, ''ye'' {{lang|okm|ㅕ}} {{IPA|[jə]}}, ''ywo'' {{lang|okm|ㅛ}} {{IPA|[jo]}} and ''ywu'' {{lang|okm|ㅠ}} {{IPA|[ju]}}.
* A ''w'' on-glide could precede ''a'' or ''e'', written with a pair of vowel symbols: ''wa'' {{lang|okm|ㅘ}} {{IPA|[wa]}} and ''we'' {{lang|okm|ㅝ}} {{IPA|[wə]}}.
* A ''y'' off-glide could follow any of the pure vowels except ''i'' or any of the six onglide-vowel combinations, and was marked by adding the letter ''i'' {{angbr|ㅣ}}. In modern Korean, the vowel-offglide sequences have become monophthongs.

Early Hangul texts distinguish three pitch contours on each syllable: low (unmarked), high (marked with one dot) and rising (marked with two dots).{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=163}} The rising tone may have been longer in duration, and is believed to have arisen from a contraction of a pair of syllables with low and high tone.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=163–165}} LMK texts do not show clear distinctions after the first high or rising tone in a word, suggesting that it was a [[pitch-accent language]] rather than having a full [[tone (linguistics)|tone]] system.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|pp=167–168}}{{sfnp|Cho|Whitman|2019|p=25}}

== Vocabulary ==

Although some Chinese words had previously entered Korean, Middle Korean was the period of the massive and systematic influx of [[Sino-Korean vocabulary]].{{sfnp|Sohn|2012|p=118}}
As a result, over half the modern Korean lexicon consists of Sino-Korean words, though they account for only about a tenth of basic vocabulary.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2000|p=136}}

[[Classical Chinese]] was the language of government and scholarship in Korea from the 7th century until the [[Gabo Reforms]] of the 1890s.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2000|pp=55–57}}
After King [[Gwangjong of Goryeo|Gwangjong]] established the ''[[gwageo]]'' civil service examinations on the Chinese model in 958, familiarity with written Chinese and the [[Chinese classics]] spread through the ruling classes.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=98}}

Korean literati read Chinese texts using a standardized Korean pronunciation, originally based on [[Middle Chinese]].
They used Chinese [[rhyme dictionary|rhyme dictionaries]], which specified the pronunciations of [[Chinese characters]] relative to other characters, and could thus be used to systematically construct a Sino-Korean reading for any word encountered in a Chinese text.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=76}}
This system became so entrenched that 15th-century efforts to reform it to more closely match the Chinese pronunciation of the time were abandoned.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2000|p=56}}

The prestige of Chinese was further enhanced by the adoption of Confucianism as the state ideology of [[Joseon]], and Chinese literary forms flooded into the language at all levels of society.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=235}}
Some of these denoted items of imported culture, but it was also common to introduce Sino-Korean words that directly competed with native vocabulary.{{sfnp|Lee|Ramsey|2011|p=235}}
Many Korean words known from Middle Korean texts have since been lost in favour of their Sino-Korean counterparts, including the following.

